Each point is one student’s own average, from the pairs we were just building by hand — now for the whole class. The red curve is a Normal(μ, σ) fitted to it. This is our assumed data-generating process (DGP).
We can now use Python to pretend that we’ve run last week’s test many times, each time drawing a new class from the same distribution.


Bessel’s correction: back in Week 1 we used n − 1, not n, to compute a standard deviation — here’s why. An unbiased estimate of the population variance divides squared deviations by n − 1, not n — the sample mean already fits this exact sample as closely as possible, so squared deviations from it slightly understate the true spread. Dividing by n − 1 corrects for that lost degree of freedom.
Békés & Kézdi’s own formulas divide by n throughout — harmless at the scale of their case studies, where the difference is vanishingly small. This module generally divides by n − 1 instead, since a class of  students is small enough that the difference is real.

