Not a coincidence — it’s a mathematical property of how OLS fits the line. It’s true by construction, for any data.

Task — compute our own R²

By hand: using Var[ŷ] and Var[y] from your own regression, compute R².



{
  const rows = student_summary.filter(d =&gt; d.finger_mm != null && !isNaN(d.finger_mm))
  if (rows.length &lt; 3) return html`&lt;p style="color:#aaa;"&gt;Not enough data yet.&lt;/p&gt;`
  const xbar = d3.mean(rows, d =&gt; d.finger_mm), ybar = d3.mean(rows, d =&gt; d.mean_rt)
  const cov = d3.mean(rows, d =&gt; (d.finger_mm - xbar) * (d.mean_rt - ybar))
  const varx = d3.mean(rows, d =&gt; (d.finger_mm - xbar) ** 2)
  const beta = cov / varx, alpha = ybar - beta * xbar
  const fitted = rows.map(d =&gt; alpha + beta * d.finger_mm)
  const varY = d3.mean(rows, d =&gt; (d.mean_rt - ybar) ** 2)
  const varYhat = d3.mean(fitted, f =&gt; (f - ybar) ** 2)
  const r2 = varYhat / varY
  return html`&lt;p style="font-family:sans-serif;font-size:1.1em;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;R² = ${r2.toFixed(3)}&lt;/strong&gt; — the regression explains about ${(r2*100).toFixed(1)}% of the variation in reaction time.&lt;/p&gt;`
}








That number is the answer to the HR question. A weak correlation (Week 3) means a low R² here — finger length barely moves the needle on reaction time. The formula exists; whether it’s useful is a separate question, R² suggested we should be skeptical.

Reaction time on device

Task — x is binary here: device (Laptop = 0, Phone = 1). Using \(\hat\beta = Cov[x,y]/Var[x]\) and \(\hat\alpha = \bar y - \hat\beta\bar x\), work out what each one must reduce to when x only ever takes the values 0 and 1.


\(\hat\alpha\) = average y when x = 0 (average reaction time on Laptop). \(\hat\beta\) = average y when x = 1, minus average y when x = 0 (Phone minus Laptop). The “line” is only ever evaluated at x=0 and x=1 — it’s really just two averages and their difference, dressed up in regression notation.



