One synthetic point — an implausible combination of very long fingers and a very fast reaction time — is enough to swing \(\hat\beta\), sometimes even flip its sign. That’s leverage: a point far from \(\bar x\) has an outsized say in where the line goes, regardless of whether it fits the pattern the rest of the data shows.

Fitting a quadratic to our own data

{
  const rows = student_summary.filter(d =&gt; d.finger_mm != null && !isNaN(d.finger_mm))
  if (rows.length &lt; 5) return html`&lt;p style="color:#aaa;"&gt;Not enough data yet.&lt;/p&gt;`
  const x = rows.map(d =&gt; d.finger_mm), y = rows.map(d =&gt; d.mean_rt)
  const n = x.length
  const s = k =&gt; d3.sum(x.map(xi =&gt; xi ** k))
  const sxy = k =&gt; d3.sum(x.map((xi, i) =&gt; xi ** k * y[i]))
  // Normal equations for y = a + b1*x + b2*x^2, solved via Cramer's rule
  const M = [[n, s(1), s(2)], [s(1), s(2), s(3)], [s(2), s(3), s(4)]]
  const v = [d3.sum(y), sxy(1), sxy(2)]
  const det3 = m =&gt; m[0][0]*(m[1][1]*m[2][2]-m[1][2]*m[2][1]) - m[0][1]*(m[1][0]*m[2][2]-m[1][2]*m[2][0]) + m[0][2]*(m[1][0]*m[2][1]-m[1][1]*m[2][0])
  const D = det3(M)
  const withCol = (m, col, vec) =&gt; m.map((row, i) =&gt; row.map((val, j) =&gt; j === col ? vec[i] : val))
  const a = det3(withCol(M, 0, v)) / D
  const b1 = det3(withCol(M, 1, v)) / D
  const b2 = det3(withCol(M, 2, v)) / D
  const fitted = x.map(xi =&gt; a + b1 * xi + b2 * xi ** 2)
  const ybar = d3.mean(y)
  const varY = d3.mean(y.map(yi =&gt; (yi - ybar) ** 2))
  const varYhat = d3.mean(fitted.map(f =&gt; (f - ybar) ** 2))
  const r2quad = varYhat / varY
  const xbar = d3.mean(x)
  const cov = d3.mean(x.map((xi, i) =&gt; (xi - xbar) * (y[i] - ybar)))
  const varx = d3.mean(x.map(xi =&gt; (xi - xbar) ** 2))
  const betaLin = cov / varx, alphaLin = ybar - betaLin * xbar
  const fittedLin = x.map(xi =&gt; alphaLin + betaLin * xi)
  const varYhatLin = d3.mean(fittedLin.map(f =&gt; (f - ybar) ** 2))
  const r2lin = varYhatLin / varY
  const grid = d3.range(d3.min(x), d3.max(x), (d3.max(x) - d3.min(x)) / 60)
  const curve = grid.map(xi =&gt; ({ finger_mm: xi, fit: a + b1 * xi + b2 * xi ** 2 }))
  const chart = Plot.plot({
    height: 420, marginLeft: 60,
    x: { label: "Finger length (mm)" }, y: { label: "Mean reaction time (ms)" },
    marks: [
      Plot.dot(rows, { x: "finger_mm", y: "mean_rt", fill: "#1a1a2e", r: 4 }),
      Plot.line(curve, { x: "finger_mm", y: "fit", stroke: "#e63946", strokeWidth: 2.5 }),
    ]
  })
  return html`&lt;div&gt;${chart}&lt;p style="font-family:sans-serif;font-size:1em;text-align:center;"&gt;Linear R² = &lt;strong&gt;${r2lin.toFixed(3)}&lt;/strong&gt; &nbsp;·&nbsp; Quadratic R² = &lt;strong&gt;${r2quad.toFixed(3)}&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;`
}







Does the quadratic noticeably beat the straight line? If the improvement is tiny, that’s a finding too — it says the relationship really is close to linear, for this data.

